Here is rnl:

int
rnl(x)          /* 0 <= rnl(x) < x; sometimes subtracting Luck */
register int x; /* good luck approaches 0, bad luck approaches (x-1) */
{
        register int i;

#ifdef DEBUG
        if (x <= 0) {
                impossible("rnl(%d) attempted", x);
                return(0);
        }
#endif
        i = RND(x);

        if (Luck && rn2(50 - Luck)) {
            i -= (x <= 15 && Luck >= -5 ? Luck/3 : Luck);
            if (i < 0) i = 0;
            else if (i >= x) i = x-1;
        }

        return i;
}

RND(10) gives a random integer from 0 to 9 inclusive.  Assuming that
your Luck was maxed out at 13, you will get a luck bonus of 4 points
36/37 of the time.  Unless the initial roll was 9, rnl(10) will then
be less than 5.  The other 1/37 of the time, the luck bonus will be
ignored, and then rnl(10) will be >= 5 half the time.  Hence the net
probability that you will NOT free yourself here is:

36/37 . 1/10 + 1/37 . 1/2 = 41 / 370 ~= 0.11.

What's more, if you have a helmet there is a 7/8 chance that it will
block any mind flayer attack that gets past this stage.

So no, mind flayers are not as terrifying as they used to be, but I
don't know whether this is intentional.
